title: "The Small Blue Quilt on the Isolette"
date: "2026-08-01"
excerpt: "On a warm Wednesday afternoon in the first week of August, in the sunlit back room of Trinity Lutheran Church on North Eighth Street in Manitowoc, Wisconsin, seven women in their sixties and seventies gather around a long oak table with a cardboard bolt of Moda flannel, a tin of common pins, and a small tan Kenmore machine a woman named Marjorie Voss has been oiling every second Tuesday since the March of 1974, and stitch — as they have stitched every Wednesday afternoon since the first Wednesday after Marjorie's daughter Lisa Marie was born ten weeks early at Holy Family Memorial on the twenty-second of February, 1974 — the fifty-first small blue crib-sized quilt of the calendar year, bound for the neonatal unit on the fourth floor of the same hospital forty-one blocks south. Somewhere on the second floor of the hospital's foundation office on South Tenth Street, on the same Wednesday afternoon at three-oh-two, the record for Marjorie L. Voss in the sustaining-donor file reads *$40/mo since 06/1974, mail only, no engagement noted, do not upgrade*. Behind the sewing machine, in a small maroon composition notebook Marjorie has kept on the shelf above the ironing board since a Wednesday in the April of 1974, is the small careful record of a half-century the foundation's file will never learn about until the March she finally, at the small pine desk in the front room, sits down to write it."
